          "provider": null,
          "prompt": "Create a second premium cinematic sneaker commercial storyboard sheet based strictly on the uploaded reference shoe image.\n\nSTRICT PRODUCT IDENTITY LOCK:\nUse the uploaded reference shoe image as the only product reference.\nThe exact same shoe must appear consistently in every panel.\nPreserve exactly:\n- silhouette\n- proportions\n- upper construction\n- panel layout\n- toe box shape\n- heel structure\n- tongue shape\n- lace style\n- sole shape\n- outsole pattern impression\n- stitching placement\n- logo placement if visible\n- all recognizable product design details\n\nSTRICT COLOR LOCK:\nKeep the product colors exactly the same as the reference image.\nPreserve the exact color blocking and exact placement of every color zone.\nDo not make the shoe brighter, darker, warmer, cooler, more saturated, or more stylized than the reference.\nThe product must remain visually identical in color across all panels, even in action shots.\n\nSTRICT MATERIAL LOCK:\nKeep the material appearance exactly consistent with the reference image.\nMaintain the true look of mesh, suede, leather, knit, rubber, foam, or any mixed-material areas.\nKeep the exact matte, semi-matte, or slightly glossy finish as shown in the reference.\nPreserve fine texture, surface grain, weave, thickness feeling, and edge definition.\n\nACTION CONSISTENCY:\nEven during movement, water splash, and low-angle shots, the product must stay recognizable and accurate.\nDo not let motion change the shoe design.\nDo not let splash or blur hide the main construction details.\nThe product should remain sharp enough to identify the true design.\n\nSTORYBOARD FORMAT:\nCreate a professional production storyboard sheet on a black editorial presentation background.\nArrange the storyboard into 7 cinematic panels in a clean 4 columns x 2 rows layout, with the last frame centered or slightly larger if needed.\nAdd small clean panel numbers 09 to 15.\nNo long captions, no messy text.\n\nVISUAL STYLE:\nPremium urban sneaker commercial, realistic product cinematography, energetic city movement, wet pavement reflections, cool neutral color grading, sharp product focus, polished but realistic, high-end commercial look.\n\nPANELS:\n09 — Walking on wet pavement:\nLow-angle tracking shot of the reference shoes walking across wet urban pavement, preserving exact color and material appearance.\n\n10 — Side motion shot:\nClose side tracking view of the shoes moving forward, with the product remaining sharp and accurate.\n\n11 — Running up stairs:\nLow-angle rear shot of the wearer running up modern city stairs, with heel, sole, and side profile clearly visible.\n\n12 — Urban wide shot:\nWide shot of the wearer moving through a modern city walkway, focus on lower body and shoes, no face shown.\n\n13 — Splash impact shot:\nHero slow-motion shot of the shoe stepping into a shallow puddle, with water splash around the sole, while the product design remains unchanged.\n\n14 — Sole macro detail:\nExtreme close-up of the outsole tread and sidewall, showing crisp technical texture and true material finish.\n\n15 — Final hero shot:\nClean side-profile product hero shot on a glossy reflective surface, with a bold premium abstract background, preserving exact product identity.\n\nLIGHTING:\nUse realistic commercial lighting that reveals true product color and material.\nAvoid strong colored lighting that changes the appearance of the shoe.\nThe lighting may be cinematic, but the product must remain visually faithful to the reference.\n\nNEGATIVE PROMPT:\nDo not redesign the shoe.\nDo not recolor the shoe.\nDo not alter the material.\nDo not change the sole.\nDo not change the toe shape.\nDo not invent new details.\nDo not add extra logos.\nDo not add fake text.\nDo not distort proportions.\nDo not make the shoe overly glossy or plastic.\nDo not create mismatched shoes.\nDo not blur away important product details.\nDo not stylize the shoe into CGI, cartoon, or concept-art form.",
