          "node_name": "Video 02 - Prompt",
          "node_type": "prompt",
          "provider": null,
          "prompt": "Frame 1 = last frame của video 01, Frame 2 = ảnh hũ mật ong đính kèm\n\nCreate one seamless 8-second photorealistic cinematic video connecting Frame 1 directly to Frame 2.\n\nFrame 1 is the exact opening frame and must continue the same bee, flight direction, camera momentum, meadow environment, lighting and visual realism established in the previous video. Frame 2 is the exact final composition reference and must be reached naturally without abruptly replacing, morphing or reconstructing the scene.\n\n0.0–4.5 seconds\n\nContinue the high-speed FPV chase flight immediately from Frame 1 with no pause and no visible transition. The same realistic honey bee flies rapidly through the remaining wildflowers, with the camera positioned close behind and slightly above it.\n\nThe bee banks naturally around several flowers, passes close to petals and leaves, then exits the dense meadow into a quiet sunlit woodland clearing. Ahead, gradually reveal the moss-covered tree stump and the honey jar from Frame 2 as a distant but clearly recognizable destination.\n\nThe jar must already possess the same physical identity as Frame 2: identical glass shape, amber honey color, metallic lid, natural rope tied around the neck, cream paper label, label placement and overall proportions. Do not introduce a different jar and transform it later.\n\nMaintain fast but controlled FPV motion, realistic foreground parallax and edge motion blur. The natural flower meadow should transition spatially into the forest clearing through continuous forward travel, not through crossfade, dissolve, teleportation or environmental morphing.\n\n4.5–6.0 seconds\n\nAs the bee approaches the stump, smoothly decelerate from hyperlapse speed into cinematic slow motion. Motion blur gradually decreases. The camera rises slightly and begins a gentle curved orbit from directly behind the bee toward a close three-quarter side angle.\n\nThis must feel like one physical camera movement: no cut, no sudden camera jump and no instant viewpoint switch. The honey jar grows steadily larger in frame through real forward movement.\n\nThe background converges naturally toward Frame 2: mossy stump in the foreground, warm forest clearing, distant mountain silhouette, soft clouds, dark evergreen trees and golden late-afternoon sunlight.\n\n6.0–8.0 seconds\n\nEnter elegant macro slow motion. The camera slowly pushes toward the honey jar while the bee extends its legs, reduces wing speed and performs one physically accurate landing on the upper edge of the metallic lid.\n\nShow the landing clearly:\nthe bee approaches, hovers briefly, extends its front legs, touches the lid, settles its remaining legs and folds its wings naturally. No bouncing, sliding or sinking into the lid.\n\nEnd with the composition aligned as closely as possible to Frame 2. Preserve the exact jar position, scale, label design, lid, rope bow, honey color, moss, stump, mountains, trees, clouds, golden lighting, depth of field and vertical framing of Frame 2. The camera finishes with a very slow cinematic push-in toward the product while the landed bee remains naturally visible on the lid.\n\nThe additional flying bees visible in Frame 2 may appear gradually in the distant background only during the final section. They must remain secondary, small and naturally placed. The main bee must remain uniquely identifiable and must not be duplicated.\n\nStrict Frame 2 preservation:\ndo not rewrite, distort or invent label text; do not change the jar shape; do not move the rope bow; do not alter the lid material; do not change the quantity or color of honey; do not replace the stump, moss, mountains, trees or clouds; do not alter the final lighting direction; do not add flowers around the jar unless already present in Frame 2.\n\nAvoid:\ncartoon, animation, 3D-rendered appearance, artificial product visualization, CGI insect, oversized bee, magical transition, crossfade, scene replacement, object morphing, jar deformation, label flicker, changing text, duplicated main bee, extra legs or wings, insect-body warping, unstable camera, excessive lens distortion, abrupt slowdown, abrupt perspective change, flickering forest, moving mountains, changing clouds, fake plastic moss, oversaturated colors, text overlays, subtitles or added graphics.",
